Build the key tables from the ASCII-capable layout while an input method is
selected.

Windows keeps the keyboard layout and the IME apart: with the Korean IME
active, VK_W still maps to 'W' (MapVirtualKey, ToUnicode) and the Hangul
composition happens inside the IME.  macOS folds both into one input source,
and the Korean input method carries a layout whose letter keys produce jamo.
winemac builds its key tables from that layout, so while Korean is selected
every letter key produces a jamo character instead of its letter.

A game does not notice the input method at all, it just stops responding:
Unreal maps the letter keys by character (FWindowsPlatformInput::GetKeyMap
lists only the special keys), so W arrives as U+3148, matches nothing and is
dropped, while Escape, arrows and function keys still work.  A movement key
held at the moment the source switches never gets its release either, because
the release maps the same way, so the character keeps walking.  Measured in
Hogwarts Legacy with a +key trace: the source flipped mid-game with W, Shift
and Space held (a sprint-jump on the input source shortcut), and from then on
"WM_CHAR ... L\"\3139\"" replaced "L\"f\"" for every press.

So while an input method is selected, use the ASCII-capable layout it sits on
(TISCopyCurrentASCIICapableKeyboardLayoutInputSource), which is what Windows
would report.  Composition is unaffected: it goes through the input method
itself (NSTextInputContext), which does not read these tables.

--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m	2026-07-15 03:37:33
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m	2026-09-02 22:05:35
@@ -127,6 +127,35 @@
 @end
 
 
+
+/* The keyboard layout Wine builds its key tables from.
+ *
+ * Windows keeps the keyboard layout and the IME apart: with the Korean IME
+ * active, VK_W still maps to 'W' (MapVirtualKey, ToUnicode) and the Hangul
+ * composition happens inside the IME.  macOS folds both into one input source,
+ * and the Korean input method carries a layout whose letter keys produce jamo.
+ * Building the key tables from that layout makes every letter key
+ * unrecognisable to a game: Unreal maps letters by character, so W arrives as
+ * U+3148 and is dropped, and a W held while the source is switched stays down
+ * because its release maps the same way.
+ *
+ * So while an input method is selected, use the ASCII-capable layout it sits
+ * on, which is what Windows would report.  Composition is unaffected: it goes
+ * through the input method itself (NSTextInputContext), not these tables. */
+static TISInputSourceRef CopyKeyboardLayoutForKeyTables(void)
+{
+    TISInputSourceRef source = TISCopyCurrentKeyboardInputSource();
+    TISInputSourceRef layout = NULL;
+    CFStringRef type = source ? TISGetInputSourceProperty(source, kTISPropertyInputSourceType) : NULL;
+
+    if (type && !CFEqual(type, kTISTypeKeyboardLayout))
+        layout = TISCopyCurrentASCIICapableKeyboardLayoutInputSource();
+    if (!layout)
+        layout = TISCopyCurrentKeyboardLayoutInputSource();
+    if (source) CFRelease(source);
+    return layout;
+}
+
 @implementation WineApplicationController
 
     @synthesize keyboardType, lastFlagsChanged;
